{"tool_name":"repliscan","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"repliscan","home":"https://github.com/zyndagj/repliscan","summary":"Tool for classifying replication timing regions.","addition_date":"2018-08-12T10:27:04Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-25T14:28:22.170270Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:repliscan","credit":[{"email":"vaughn@tacc.utexas.edu","name":"Matthew W. Vaughn","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://github.com/zyndagj/repliscan/blob/master/README.md"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Classification","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2990"}]}],"name":"Repliscan","operating_system":["Linux","Mac"],"owner":"migle_sur","publication":[{"doi":"10.1186/s12859-017-1774-x","metadata":{"abstract":"Background: Replication timing experiments that use label incorporation and high throughput sequencing produce peaked data similar to ChIP-Seq experiments. However, the differences in experimental design, coverage density, and possible results make traditional ChIP-Seq analysis methods inappropriate for use with replication timing. Results: To accurately detect and classify regions of replication across the genome, we present Repliscan. Repliscan robustly normalizes, automatically removes outlying and uninformative data points, and classifies Repli-seq signals into discrete combinations of replication signatures. The quality control steps and self-fitting methods make Repliscan generally applicable and more robust than previous methods that classify regions based on thresholds. Conclusions: Repliscan is simple and effective to use on organisms with different genome sizes. Even with analysis window sizes as small as 1 kilobase, reliable profiles can be generated with as little as 2.4x coverage.","authors":[{"name":"Concia L."},{"name":"Hanley-Bowdoin L."},{"name":"Song J."},{"name":"Thompson W.F."},{"name":"Vaughn M.W."},{"name":"Wear E.E."},{"name":"Zynda G.J."}],"citationCount":12,"date":"2017-08-07T00:00:00Z","journal":"BMC Bioinformatics","title":"Repliscan: A tool for classifying replication timing regions"},"pmcid":"PMC5547489","pmid":"28784090","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"DNA replication and recombination","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3127"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"Repliscan","home":"https://bio.tools/repliscan","summary":"Tool for classifying replication timing regions.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:repliscan/cmd/github.com","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"binaries":{"freeness":null,"registration":null},"packages":null,"source_packages":false,"sourcecode":{"free":null,"interpreted":true,"license":null,"public":true,"readme":false,"repository":{"anonymous":null,"online":null}}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"general":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":14,"citations":[{"count":1,"year":"2017"},{"count":1,"year":"2023"},{"count":1,"year":"2024"},{"count":2,"year":"2018"},{"count":2,"year":"2020"},{"count":2,"year":"2025"},{"count":2,"year":"2026"},{"count":3,"year":"2022"}],"doi":"10.1186/s12859-017-1774-x","pmcid":"PMC5547489","pmid":"28784090","ref_count":27,"refs":[{"count":1,"year":"1978"},{"count":1,"year":"1993"},{"count":1,"year":"2005"},{"count":1,"year":"2011"},{"count":1,"year":"2012"},{"count":1,"year":"2013"},{"count":1,"year":"2016"},{"count":2,"year":"2002"},{"count":2,"year":"2003"},{"count":2,"year":"2004"},{"count":2,"year":"2007"},{"count":2,"year":"2009"},{"count":2,"year":"2010"},{"count":2,"year":"2014"},{"count":3,"year":"2008"},{"count":3,"year":"2015"}],"title":"Repliscan: a tool for classifying replication timing regions.","year":2017}]}],"readme":false,"summary":